## Vector 2: HBNC Toxic Conditions — What The Official Record Shows

- **Navigation Center site (17631 Cameron Lane / 17642 Beach Blvd):** Legal filings have emerged alleging residents were exposed to a hazardous waste plume containing lead, arsenic, and hexavalent chromium. Operational activities alleged to have mobilized these contaminants.
- **Prior abandoned landfill site (Gothard Street, 2018):** A prior proposed homeless shelter site was abandoned after community backlash — the site was a former landfill (1947–1982) documented to contain methane and other hazardous gases.
- **Hydrogen sulfide odor events:** Documented separate from the Navigation Center — coastal/wetland origin, monitored by SCAQMD. Low-level H2S confirmed causing headaches, nausea, throat irritation.
- **Key Open Question:** The legal filings alleging lead/arsenic/hexavalent chromium exposure at the actual Navigation Center site require extraction and documentation. This is the strongest vector for a federal environmental exposure claim.

